00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.950
So we have another very good
friend on the show helping out.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.330
Lawrence Zarian is in the heart of it all,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.593
along with Maria Sanson in
the streets here in Boston

00:00:48.593 --> 00:00:49.633
(indistinct) today.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.210
What's up y'all?

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.260
- Hey, Kelly, how you doing?

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.420
So we're getting this party started today.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.960
Where else but the Boston
Tea Party Ships and Museum

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.740
because there is so much history here LZ

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.310
where we are standing right now.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.050
This is where the actual

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.940
Boston Tea Party took
place nearly 250 years ago.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.773
- [Kelly] Oh yeah,

00:01:10.773 --> 00:01:13.350
- we are surrounded by
these full scale replicas

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.920
of the 18th century ships.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.030
You've got the Beaver and Eleanor.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.610
There are live actors that
you can interact with.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.760
And my favorite part you guys right here,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.240
LZ got to check it out,
you go on the ship,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.250
and you actually throw the tea overboard.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.907
So he was literally spilling
the tea in our Boston Harbor.

00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:31.170
(Lawrence laughs)
He's very good at that,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.060
sort of.
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.680
- You looked very athletic, I feel.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.970
So Lawrence, what have
you been doing out there?

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.330
- You know what, I've been keeping busy.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.220
Number one, I've been reunited
with my friend Maria Sanson,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.930
we've known each other-
- So thank you for that.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.870
- We've known each other
for a really long time.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.027
Number two, I've been
staying outta trouble.

00:01:48.027 --> 00:01:48.860
- No.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.693
- Lying.
- Lying.

00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:51.200
- And number three, you know what I did?

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.870
I had some fun and went
to the Freedom Trail.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.760
It is this 2.5 mile historic walk

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.470
through some of Boston's
most historic landmarks.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.030
From Boston Common, Paul Revere's house,

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.310
and you know what I loved?

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.340
I loved the Massachusetts Statehouse

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.420
with the famous golden dome.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.280
- Gorgeous.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.640
- That was super fun,
everybody should do that.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.560
- Yeah, it's got the famous scaffolding

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.330
right in front of it right now,

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:18.730
it's amazing.
(Kelly laughing)

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.258
That was amazing.
- You heard that.

00:02:20.258 --> 00:02:21.480
It's very old.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.210
- I know, that's what I
love about Boston, though.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.660
It's so beautiful, and I grew up in Texas

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.550
where nothing is like that
old, it's not that good.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.410
It looks like you're in Europe,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.420
like if you're from Texas.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.550
So Lawrence, so have you gotten
a taste of the local flavor?

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.800
- You know what, actually I have.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.930
Now did you know, Kelly, that Boston

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.570
is the home to America's
oldest restaurant?

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.810
It is called the Union Oyster House,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.690
and what I love about it is when you walk

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.680
into this restaurant, you are
stepping back into history.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.620
The stalls and the oyster bar are actually

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.450
in its original locations.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.427
And for me personally,
what I loved, I got to sit

00:02:58.427 --> 00:03:01.740
on John F. Kennedy's favorite booth.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.610
- [Maria] That's so cool.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.040
- That was awesome.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.430
- I love oysters, my mouth is watering.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.930
So wait, is there anywhere
else that we should know about

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.040
if we go there?

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.700
- Oh, you know what I stumbled upon?

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.750
I stumbled upon Faneuil Hall.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.490
It is this great little location,

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.950
it's been around since 1742.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.940
They've got amazing shops,
delicious restaurants

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.440
but also what they have is
they have this brand new

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.470
interactive, dinosaur exhibit,
it's called Dino Safari.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.540
And everybody should come
here and bring the kids.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.080
They have actually 30
life sized dinosaurs,

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.400
and real actual relics.
(Maria groans)

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.593
And I thought that Maria and I were old.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.551
- Speak for yourself.

00:03:44.551 --> 00:03:45.900
I'm a young lady.
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.570
- You gotta see these
relics and the dinosaurs.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.690
- My son would actually
love that, he's so bad.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.540
But that exhibits here
through the summertime,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.590
all the way through September,

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.260
so plenty of time to check it out.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.130
- Oh, my kids would freak out over that.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.670
Well, it looks like y'all are
living it up in the Northeast.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.780
I'm so glad y'all got reunited,
what are you doing Lawrence?

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.332
- Hold on Kelly, I've got
one more thing, Kelly.

00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:10.165
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:04:10.165 --> 00:04:13.930
- As you know, I'm a slave to fashion,

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.100
and when you are on Boston,
you have a little cheddar.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:17.933
- Oh my God.

00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:20.037
- And you wear your lobster sweater.

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:22.600
- Wicked good.
- Wicked smile.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.980
- I love that the entire interview,

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.010
you have been looking
forward to that moment,

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:28.602
I love you so much.

00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:30.452
- It is the big reveal.
- Thank you Lawrence,

00:04:30.452 --> 00:04:31.789
